commentBody:
@Matt

I have never read a single comment in this forum that so much as implied a criticism of anyone’s “intelligence” or “character” on the basis of their not being able to solve a puzzle.

Btw nice humble brag about how your life is too enriched with Truly valuable things for you to spend any significant amount of time on crossword puzzles…on a crossword puzzle forum 🤣

@RA jones

Like some other internet curiosities, this term was supposedly created by a winner mistyping "own" to show dominance.  That was quickly taken up by the crowd, either sarcastically or in homage, and became common usage.

A similar thing happened in the market when a previously hot stock was dropping.   A defender was quick to recommend that people not panic and to HOLD the stock but typed it as HODL.  That also was picked up and reused by the community.

@George
The why is there a movie Mothra vs Godzilla?

Although there’s also a movie Superman vs. Batman and I wouldn’t call them adversaries either. But I do believe in that movie at least Mothra was an adversary  of Godzilla.

@Mark

I won’t debate the general point as to whether complaints about puzzles are tedious.

But if your self-conversation here has proved anything in that regard, it pertains specifically to a genre of complaints that come from a place of partial understanding — that is, when solvers criticize a puzzle because it left them feeling less clever than they hoped/expected, but rather than simply acknowledging that, they look outwards to find fault with the puzzle.

@Matt

You and I are in the same page today, which makes me feel better because I thought it was a decent challenge as well, so I’m not just losing my touch.

Shana Tovah to everyone on Spring Hill.

@Matt
@SP

Ok, have we gone through the bizarro mirror or something?

I actually thought it was fairly easy tonight.  Sure, there were places when my puzzie sense seemed to hone in on the trick quickly (ORALB for [Toothbrush handle?], REPO for [Acquire by default?], ERASES for [Takes marks off in a classroom?]).  

I almost think a puzzle that *wasn't* trying to trick me might be trickier for me now.

But this is very much a "pride goeth before a fall" situation, so I'd keep my piehole shut if I had any sense.

@Francis

For our international solvers, perhaps old Sam isn't fair game for the puzzle, but well-read Americans should be familiar with the names of the Supremes.

Especially these days.

@lucky13

😂😂😂  Exactly.  

An oval can't be round because it's OVAL!

@Francis

See definition 2b:

<a href="https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/round" target="_blank">https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/round</a>

@NanuNanu

The earth is round; but it's not OVAL. I have an OVAL face; but it's not round.

I'm not an expert, but, BTW, I believe that the earth's path/orbit is OVAL or more precisely "elliptical."

@Wyra

I used to be very uneasy around spiders but then a really huge one spun his or her web in our bedroom window. I quickly realized it was the perfect protection against mosquitos and other bugs. My wife and I named the spider Bernard (or Bernardyna) and considered him/her part of the household. But then one day a bird snatched Bernard... I didn't have it in me to remove the ever more tatty web from the window for weeks!

Anyway, I've actually liked spiders since then.

@Andrzej

That's a lovely story, thank you. I've tried very hard over the years to make peace with spiders. I love orb weavers in the fall and I intellectually think all spiders are super cool and helpful. I can even relocate small ones on my own and leave tiny ones be.

But this one is so big. So, so big. My lizard brain is terrified and I can't rationalize my way out of it. I still don't want to kill it, and if I could find a way to be friends I would. But Big Bertha here needs her own county.

@Andrzej
As a child of weavers, I was forbidden to kill a spider; as you say they keep moths, flies and other bugs down. It was a sacking offence in the mill to kill one. There is a particular type of moth, I forget the name, that could decimate a wool sack in days if not found.
To this day I won’t kill a spider. I’m happy to evict all but the biggest ones; those I leave to DH. Now, he has a similar thing with rats, something to do with growing up on a farm. He’s happy to live and let live as long as they don’t burrow into the house walls. But the way the jump scares the living daylights out of me.

@Steve L
They kept other vermin like stoats out of the feed bins and chicken runs. Rats do a lot of damage, but stoats are destruction zones, including killing as many chickens as a fox. Rats are more likely to be injured or killed by chickens. We once found a dead rat in the roosting space of our henhouse, literally pecked to death. Those girls play mean,
He’s more amenable to ahem removing rats now, especially when I start screaming.

@Marc A. Leaf

That sounds yummy. I'll have to try it. 
I use the blender to mix powdered milk and water and it is frothy just like at Starbucks!

@Mark et alia

What about good ole PDQ???

I learned that from my Great-aunt Pearl Waddell, a pediatrician and OB-GYN.  (She had to go to Cisco, TX, to find a place willing to have a female resident--1930's...  but then the advent of WWII meant that many male MDs were drafted.  She was recruited by a city in Arkansas, where she practiced for more than 25 years.)  She delivered quite a few of our classmates...

@Bruce
Way back when there were barely any tv channels, those movies were on one of them quite frequently, probably on Saturdays, and my younger brother would watch them and because we kids were in the living room together with the only tv, my sister and I wound up watching, too. 
I don't remember Gamera from back then, and if I had, I had MOTHRA's H from HAND DRYER already.

@Frank Sartori
When I started solving on paper, I probably spent longer trying to figure out how the clues fit the answers in the answer key than I had on my solving attempts.

Well, maybe not. I often spent a couple of weeks on a puzzle. But figuring out how NYT clues fit the answers was an education of a different stripe than the crosswords I had worked before.

@Sparrow 

I held onto HOUSE like Gollum clenching the Ring, until the sheer weight of evidence finally ripped it away.

Way too easy for a Saturday clue, so I should have known better.

@Suzanne 

I've always heard it as "Now I know."

It's probably one of those regional things whereby if you ask someone a dozen or so of them you can pin down what part of the country they grew up in.

@P-Town Forever
I liked the clue, though! 

Hadn't heard of the book, not even sure of its stance, but what a title.

@Mean Old Lady

Each time you order a burrito at my favorite place, they STAMP your CARD.

When you fill it you get a free burrito.

@Kevin D
Oh yeah. 
Guess my synapses have not been activated enough today.

@Kevin D
It finally hit me--I think of punch cards as what we used to feed into computers. Is that what you meant?

Not the kind a clerk would use a hole punch on each time you made a purchase?

@HeathieJ

Make sure you check the bands for any signs of stress before using them each day.  

I used to check mine every once in a while, but a couple of years ago, I was doing fast side stepping with a band around my ankles and my band snapped.  I hit the floor really hard and landed right on my trochanter.  Luckily, I didn't injure myself, but I had a giant bruise that lasted for months.
